module gunbc.v1_interpreter_primitive_surface

data v1_interpreter_primitive_surface_authority_note: String = "THE V1 INTERPRETER'S PRIMITIVE DISPATCH SURFACE, MADE ENUMERABLE. Before this carrier the surface had no roster anywhere: its only authority was string match arms inside the hand-maintained seed src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs, so 'which primitives does the interpreter provide' was a question no consumer could ask. That is why it was the fifth surface the primitive-identity census could not read, and why v1 deletion could not be sequenced honestly: you cannot show that nothing still needs a capability you cannot enumerate.\n\nTHE SURFACE IS SIX DISPATCH FUNCTIONS, NOT ONE. Measured against the tree rather than assumed: eval_builtin_inner, eval_algebra_method_inner, eval_call (a native fold intercept that fires BEFORE the free-call dispatch, which is why fold_list never reaches the builtin registry), try_v2_std_collection_map_primitive_grounding, try_parse_table_memo_dispatch, and a lookup short-circuit inside eval_method_call. A census that reads only eval_builtin_inner sees about two thirds of the arms.\n\nSERVICE OPERATIONS ARE NOT ARMS, and that is a finding rather than an omission. eval_service_call resolves Service.Op against ctx.service_ops, which is built from the DECLARED .dag corpus, so that population is already enumerable as data and is deliberately outside this roster's denominator. The interpreter contributes exactly one service-name dispatch of its own -- the Filesystem.Read hermetic checkout carve-out -- plus transport realizations, which dispatch on transport and HTTP method values rather than on primitive names. Only the carve-out is a row here.\n\nHOW EACH FORM IS KNOWN, WHICH IS THE FRONTIER. FreeCall rows from eval_builtin_inner are DERIVED: that arm list is now a single macro authority in v1_interpreter.rs from which both the dispatch and this roster expand, so an arm without a roster row and a roster row without an arm are both unwritable rather than reconciled afterwards (DESIGN 5, construction over validation). No live-tree scan of Rust source is involved and none is wanted: a regex interpreting hand-written match arms would leave the denominator in Rust while adding a second thing that can be wrong.\n\nTHE REMAINING ROWS ARE DECLARED BY HAND and can therefore go stale. That is counted (v1_interpreter_declared_arm_count) rather than hidden, and the dissolution trigger is specific, not aspirational: apply the v1_builtin_arms macro shape to eval_algebra_method_inner (uniform literal arms today, so it is the same mechanical transformation), then to the three small dispatches, then delete each declared block as its projection lands. The Filesystem.Read carve-out is the one row that stays declared until the hermetic carve-out itself dissolves. Absence from a declared block is NOT evidence that an arm does not exist.\n\nROWS CARRY THE ARM'S OWN FACTS ONLY. There is deliberately no primitive-identity field, not even an optional one: an arm that joins to no known primitive must remain representable, because that population is exactly what a primitive-identity join needs to discover. A roster whose shape forbade recording an unjoined arm would be complete by construction and useless as a denominator. This carrier answers WHICH ARMS EXIST; which primitive each one realizes belongs to the join.\n\nONE ROW PER (FORM, SPELLING); ALIASES SHARE AN ARM IDENTITY. length, count and size are one arm under three rows, so they cannot disagree by construction. A spelling appearing under two different arm identities in the SAME form is unreachable code, because the earlier arm always wins. A spelling appearing under two different FORMS is two genuine implementations: lookup is the live specimen, dispatched both by the eval_method_call short-circuit and by an algebra arm the short-circuit shadows."

data v1_interpreter_source_path: String = "src/v1/stage0/src/v1_interpreter.rs"

type InterpreterDispatchForm
  = FreeCall
  | MethodCall
  | ServiceDispatch
  | NativeSpecial

type InterpreterPrimitiveArmId {
  identity: NonEmptyStr
}

-- What shape of call an arm accepts. Modelled as a coproduct rather than an Int
-- because it is NOT derivable from the dispatch today: arm bodies reach for
-- positional.first(), positional.as_slice() patterns, and byte-vector coercions,
-- so any arity written here would be a hand transcription that rots silently the
-- first time a body changes. A typed absence is countable; a fabricated number is
-- not. Trigger that makes it derivable: the R1 step where the macro arm list
-- carries an explicit parameter spec beside each body, at which point arity
-- expands from the same tokens as the spelling and this variant gains real ones.
type ArmAcceptedShape
  = ShapeNotDerivableFromDispatch

type InterpreterPrimitiveDispatchArm {
  arm: InterpreterPrimitiveArmId
  form: InterpreterDispatchForm
  authored_spelling: NonEmptyStr
  realization_module: NonEmptyStr
  dispatch_symbol: NonEmptyStr
  accepted_shape: ArmAcceptedShape
}

type ArmEnumeration = DerivedFromDispatch | DeclaredHere

-- DERIVED. Projected from the interpreter's own arm list; no spelling is
-- transcribed, so this projection cannot disagree with the dispatch it describes.
fn v1_interpreter_free_call_arms() -> List<InterpreterPrimitiveDispatchArm> {
  interpreter_free_call_arm_rows()
    |> map(r => InterpreterPrimitiveDispatchArm {
      arm: InterpreterPrimitiveArmId { identity: r.arm_identity },
      form: FreeCall,
      authored_spelling: r.authored_spelling,
      realization_module: "v1_interpreter", dispatch_symbol: "eval_builtin_inner", accepted_shape: ShapeNotDerivableFromDispatch,
    })
}

-- DECLARED. Trigger: the v1_builtin_arms macro shape applied to
